-- Passage rg9-connections-p01
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('103bf515-84ed-5044-9728-ec942fd656a4', 'rg9-connections-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-connections'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Map of Broken Streets$q$, $q$For years, the city of Brennick fixed its potholes in a simple way. A road crew drove a set route each week and patched whatever holes they happened to see. Holes on busy streets were usually found quickly, but holes on quiet side streets could go unnoticed for months.

In 2021, a high school student named Tomasz Adeyemi-Lund noticed that the pothole outside his grandmother's house had been there all winter. He wondered how many other holes the crews were missing. For a computer science project, he built a simple phone app that let residents take a photo of a pothole and mark its location on a map.

At first, only Tomasz's classmates used the app. Then a local radio host mentioned it on the morning show, and within a month residents had reported more than two thousand potholes. The map filled with colored dots, and a clear pattern appeared. The neighborhoods on the city's east side had far more unrepaired holes than anyone had realized, because the crews' weekly route rarely passed through them.

The city council invited Tomasz to present his map at a public meeting. After seeing the data, the council voted to replace the fixed route with a new system. Crews now receive a daily list of reported holes, sorted by how long each one has been waiting.

The change has had effects beyond the roads themselves. Because residents can watch their reports turn from red to green on the map, many say they trust the city's road department more than before. The department, in turn, now uses the reports to decide which streets need full repaving rather than another patch.$q$, 'draft')
