-- Passage rg9-connections-p02
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('2761433a-9fd9-5935-80ea-ff9f82419991', 'rg9-connections-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-connections'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Night the Road Closed$q$, $q$Every spring, on the first rainy night when the air stays above about seven degrees Celsius, the spotted salamanders of Hollin Ridge begin to move. They leave the hillside forest where they spend most of the year and crawl downhill toward a cluster of shallow ponds to lay their eggs.

The trouble is that Larch Road runs between the forest and the ponds. For decades, nobody paid much attention to what happened there. Then, in 2015, a retired science teacher named Delphine Okafor began walking the road with a flashlight on migration nights. She counted the salamanders that crossed safely and those that were struck by cars. In her first season, she found that nearly half of the animals she counted did not make it across.

Okafor shared her counts with the town's conservation committee. The committee members were surprised, but they also knew that closing a road is not a small request. Larch Road was a shortcut for many drivers. So the committee proposed a limited plan: the road would close only on the few rainy nights each spring when the salamanders were moving, and volunteers would set up signs and barriers.

The plan passed by a narrow vote. On the first closed night, twenty volunteers with flashlights counted more than four hundred salamanders crossing, and none were lost. Drivers who had complained found that the detour added only about four minutes to their trip.

The closures have now continued for nine years. Biologists from a nearby college report that the egg masses in the ponds have roughly doubled. Several other towns have asked Hollin Ridge for advice, and the volunteer crew, which began with one person and a flashlight, now has more than a hundred members.$q$, 'draft')
