-- Passage rg9-connections-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('24da97ea-e3ee-52ee-b168-5c446c93076a', 'rg9-connections-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-connections'), null,
        '9-12', $q$What the Lichens Knew$q$, $q$Lichens are flat, crusty growths that cling to tree bark, stones and old fences. Each one is a partnership between a fungus and a tiny green alga. Because lichens have no roots, they take in water and nutrients straight from the air, and that makes them unusually sensitive to what the air contains.

In the early 1900s, the river town of Esker Falls was home to several coal-burning mills. The smoke they released was rich in a gas called sulfur dioxide. When it mixed with rain, it formed a weak acid that soaked into the lichens on trees around town. Over a few decades, the leafy, branching lichens vanished from Esker Falls almost entirely. Only a thin gray crust survived on a few walls.

For a long time, no one connected the missing lichens to the smoke. That changed in 1962, when a botanist named Ruth Vasquez-Hale mapped lichens in a wide circle around the town. She found a striking pattern: the closer a tree stood to the mills, the fewer kinds of lichen grew on it. Her map became one of the first pieces of evidence that the air in Esker Falls was harming living things.

In the 1980s, new rules required the mills to filter their smoke, and two of them closed. Levels of sulfur dioxide dropped quickly. The lichens took longer. They grow only a few millimeters a year, so it was nearly two decades before the leafy kinds reappeared on trees near the old mill sites.

Today, students at Esker Falls High School repeat Vasquez-Hale's survey every five years. Their maps show lichens creeping steadily back toward the center of town, a slow, living record of cleaner air.$q$, 'draft')
