-- Passage rg9-connections-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('29958c5d-3f0b-5b06-adaf-2e0e1382aeae', 'rg9-connections-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-connections'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Bridge That Swayed$q$, $q$The Saltmere Footbridge opened on a bright morning in June, connecting two neighborhoods on opposite banks of the Corran River. It was long, light and elegant, and thousands of people crowded onto it for the opening celebration.

Within an hour, something strange began to happen. The bridge started to sway gently from side to side. Engineers later explained what was going on. When a bridge moves even slightly, people walking on it shift their weight to keep their balance. Without realizing it, many walkers fell into step with the swaying. Their footsteps then pushed the bridge a little farther in the same direction, which made it sway more, which made even more people fall into step.

The city closed the bridge two days after it opened. Some residents called it a failure, and newspapers printed cartoons of people clinging to the railings. The lead engineer, Priya Okoye-Brandt, insisted that the bridge was never in danger of falling. Even so, she agreed that a bridge that made people uneasy was not doing its job.

Rather than rebuild the bridge, her team decided to add dampers, which are devices that absorb motion the way shock absorbers do in a car. They tested a small model first, then placed forty dampers beneath the deck. When the bridge reopened eighteen months later, crowds walked across it with no noticeable sway.

The story did not end there. Engineers around the world studied what had happened at Saltmere, and many building guides now require designers of long footbridges to test how crowds of walkers will affect them. A bridge that was once mocked has helped make other bridges steadier.$q$, 'draft')
